.TH MADCIDE 1 "2026-10-03" "madc" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
madcide \- terminal IDE built on the live madc compiler
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B madcide
.RI [ file ]
.RI [ ro ]
.RB [ \-\-profile
.IR name ]
.RB [ \-\-std= \fIstd\fR]
.RB [ \-\-gui | \-\-tui | \-\-line ]
.br
.B madcide \-\-build\-plugin
.I dir
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B madcide
is a terminal IDE whose core is the running
.BR madc (1)
compiler itself, not an external process. The buffer holds a persistent
parse handle: the diagnostics pane, the symbol outline, and the
enclosing-function indicator in the status line are projections of live
compiler data, and syntax colour comes from real parse spans classified
through a colour scheme. Checking a file (^K ;) refreshes the same
handle \(em there is no separate "run the compiler" step.
.PP
Keybindings are data, not code: a
.I .keys
profile file maps key sequences to editor actions, and the shipped
profiles are complete personalities \(em
.B joe
(the default: the full JOE/WordStar chord set),
.BR emacs ,
.BR pico ,
and
.B neovim
(vi modal editing: normal/insert scopes, counts, operators, and the
colon line). Swap keymaps live from the options screen (^T), or toggle
vi modal editing from the modes palette (^N). Colour schemes
.RI ( .theme
files) swap the same way (^K T).
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I file
The file to edit. A path that does not exist opens as a new file.
Without a file,
.B madcide
opens an untitled buffer: saving it asks for a file name, and F5 runs it
under the name
.BR untitled .
The one-shot and server modes
.RB ( \-c ,
.BR \-\-mcp ,
.BR \-\-lsp ,
.BR \-\-serve )
need a file.
.TP
.I ro
The word
.B ro
after the file opens the buffer read-only.
.PP
Options may come before or after the file;
.B \-\-
ends them (for a file whose name starts with
.BR \- ).
An unknown option is refused with the usage (exit status 2).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-profile " name"
The profile to open under: a bundle naming the keybindings, layout,
menu, colour scheme and status line, and its settings' defaults.
Without the option, a program invoked under the name of a profile opens
under that profile: a link named
.B chthonia
to
.B madcide
(or a copy of the binary with that name) needs no option. Otherwise the
default is the
.I profile
setting in
.IR settings.json ,
else
.BR default .
.B chthonia
is the learning profile: its own keys (the Chthonia key style), the editor
with the REPL below it, and a beginner's menu.
.TP
.B \-\-gui
Open in a window (the platform webview).
.TP
.B \-\-tui
Open in the terminal.
.B madcide
does without an option;
.BR chthonia ,
the learning IDE built as its own program on madcide's base, opens a
window without one.
.TP
.B \-\-line
The line editor: commands from standard input, the screen's text to
standard output.
.TP
.BI \-\-std= std
The language standard every buffer compiles under (its diagnostics, outline,
colours, Build and Run) and the REPL runs: a standard
.BR madc (1)
names canonically \(em
.BR c17 ,
.BR c++20 ,
.BR madc .
Without the option, the
.I std
setting in
.I settings.json
decides; without that, each file's own family does: a
.I .c
file is C17 (and its REPL a C one), a
.I .cpp
file C++17, a
.I .mad
file madc. Run \(-> Language\(hellip (the
.B language
command) changes it in a running session, re-parses every buffer, restarts
the REPL and keeps the choice in
.IR settings.json ;
its
.B By file
row returns to each file's own.
.TP
.BI \-\-build\-plugin " dir"
Build the plugin in
.I dir
into its library,
.IR dir / name .so
beside its manifest, and exit: 0 when built, 1 with the compiler's
diagnostics when not. The running madcide compiles it in-process, with
its own plugin headers on the include path. Nothing is ever built
automatically.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print the usage and exit.
.SH KEYS
Under the default JOE profile,
.B ^K H
shows the help screen \(em projected from the loaded profile, so it is
always the truth for the active keymap. Headline chords:
.B ^K S
save,
.B ^K X
save and exit,
.B ^K Q
quit,
.B ^K F
find (\fB^L\fR find next),
.B ^_
undo /
.B ^^
redo,
.B ^K B
/
.B ^K K
block begin/end (\fB^K C\fR/\fB^K M\fR/\fB^K Y\fR copy/move/delete),
.B ^K E
open another file,
.B ^K O
/
.B ^K N
/
.B ^K P
split/next/previous window,
.B ^K ;
check the program,
.B ^K I
outline,
.B ^K A
cycle the code view (original/MC11/C11, plus the C++ reverse render on
a C/C++ buffer),
.B ^K Z
shell,
.B ^T
options,
.B ^N
the modes palette.
.SH FILES
Keybinding profiles, colour schemes, and the status-line layout load
from the first of these directories that exists:
.PP
.nf
    <source dir>/profiles          (running from a madc source tree)
    <bindir>/../share/madcide/profiles   (installed layout)
    <bindir>/profiles              (archive layout, data beside the binary)
.fi
.PP
.I *.keys
files are keybinding personalities;
.I *.theme
files are colour schemes;
.I joe.status
is the status-line layout. All are plain text \(em copy and edit them
to make your own.
.PP
A profile is a plugin directory,
.IR name / name .plugin
(JSON) beside the data files it carries, found first in the user's
.I plugins
directory and then in the shipped one (a
.I plugins
directory beside
.I profiles
above), so a user's profile overrides a shipped one of the same name.
The user's configuration directory holds
.I settings.json
and
.IR plugins :
.PP
.nf
    $MADCIDE_CONFIG_DIR            (when set)
    $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/madcide
    %APPDATA%\emadcide              (Windows)
    ~/.config/madcide
.fi
.PP
.I settings.json
is a JSON object:
.B "profile"
names the profile,
.B "plugins"
lists plugins whose code loads beside the profile's, and any other key is
a setting, which beats the profile's own default (for example
.BR "std" ,
the language standard; see
.BR \-\-std= ).
.PP
A plugin with code names its source in its manifest
.RB ( """code"": ""name.mad""" ),
madc code that includes
.B <madcide/plugin>
and defines
.BR madcide_plugin_activate ,
which registers its commands and views. Its library, built by
.BR \-\-build\-plugin ,
loads when the profile in use is the plugin or
.B "plugins"
names it, before the keys and menus load, so they can bind its commands.
A missing library, a library built against another plugin API version,
and an activation that refuses are each reported on the status line, and
the editor opens without the plugin.
